{"t": 1795430109.2956247, "kind": "ask", "message": "Since you drive for a delivery app, could you give a rough figure for what your household brought in from work last year, before taxes? Even a rough guess in a range helps: none; $1 to $10,000; $10,000 to $20,000; $20,000 to $30,000; $30,000 to $40,000; $40,000 to $50,000; $50,000 to $60,000; $60,000 to $80,000; $80,000 to $100,000; $100,000 to $150,000; or $150,000 or more. Or you can say you'd rather not share it.", "about": ["earned_income"], "reply": "We're pretty comfortable."}
{"t": 1795430112.0158207, "kind": "submit", "programmes": ["school_meals", "ctc", "medicaid", "snap", "eitc", "aca_ptc"], "unknown": [], "hand_off": true, "summary": "Michigan household of 5 (2 adults, 3 children, one under 5), homeowners with no rent or mortgage, assets under $2,000, one adult self-employed (delivery app driver). No pregnancy/infant, no one 65+. The household would not give a yearly earnings range, and whether anyone has a disability and whether anyone has employer insurance are unknown, so the expected cost of mistakes is still about $4,700. Recommend applying for: free or reduced-price school meals (likely), and checking Medicaid, the child tax credit (refundable part), SNAP, the earned income tax credit, and the ACA premium tax credit. A caseworker should confirm income, since it drives most of these results. WIC, CHIP, TANF and SSI look unlikely on current information.", "asked": ["earned_income", "employer_insurance", "housing_cost", "tenure", "child_under5", "infant_or_pregnant", "disabled", "senior", "assets", "college", "self_employed"]}
